{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790477832,"nonce":"5Fa2bRYtemGisegDopdkUavXL+I=","title":"Compiler: the error-flag pass slows the float speed kernel's mean","message":"NUC A/B, 5 rounds, base zypy-1faeba4d, B zypy-338986c9 (error_flag pass on): float mean 8.76 (8.6-9.5) -\u003e 10.10 (9.8-10.6) ms, wall 106.4 -\u003e 113.1 ms. The same run: scimark_fft mean 395.7 -\u003e 348.8 ms, richards and spectral-norm flat, nqueens wall 5007 -\u003e 5136 (ranges overlap). The first build (b959d92a, summaries over every module function) also slowed spectral-norm; restricting summaries to callees of bodies that name the flag fixed that.\n\nThe float HIR gets strictly smaller (Point.normalize loses its merge block, flag load and branch), so the loss looks like compile time: a debug run of the kernel does 18 optimisation rounds with the pass against 13 without, since each fold leaves a constant brcond for the next round's const_fold. Next step: make the branch unconditional inside the pass (as branch_fold does, dropping the dead edge's phi incomings) and count only threadings as restructuring, so a fold does not buy another round; then re-run float, nqueens and scimark_fft.\n\nDisable with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_ERROR_FLAG=1 meanwhile.","files":null}]}